{
 "id": "2024-kim-erosion",
 "name": "Erosion Control Dam Exteriors",
 "description": "Faithful replication of the viewing task and semantic differential ratings in Kim, Y.-R., Kim, B.-S., Kim, C., Lee, S. I., Cho, W.-S., Kweon, H., Song, C., & Seo, J. I. (2024). Evaluation of landscape created by erosion control dam using physiological and psychological indicators. PLOS ONE, 19, e0309804. https://doi.org/10.1371/journal.pone.0309804\n\nWhat participants do: participants see three pictures of the same mountain stream with an erosion control dam faced in exposed concrete, attached stone or attached wood (the authors kept everything else identical in Photoshop). The three pictures come in random order. Before each picture a grey screen is shown for one minute, then the picture for one minute. After each picture participants rate their impression on six adjective pairs on a 7-point scale from -3 to +3: natural–artificial, clean–dirty, friendly–unfriendly, calm–dynamic, peaceful–unpeaceful and relaxed–anxious. Then age, sex and whether they have professional training in erosion control or landscape design.\n\nOriginal finding (34 Korean university students, lab): participants felt the concrete-exposed and stone-attached dams as artificial and the wood-attached dam as natural; responses to all three exteriors were positive overall. Heart rate was lower when viewing the wood-attached dam than the concrete one, and vigour rose and total mood disturbance fell for the wood dam.\n\nStimuli: the three panels of Figure 2 of the article (CC BY 4.0), used with credit to the authors. This template is removed if the authors object.\n\nDifferences from the original study: (1) Online instead of a lab with a 4K display at 1.4 m; heart rate and heart rate variability are not measured. (2) The Korean brief Profile of Mood States (K-POMS-B, 30 items) is left out because it is a copyrighted instrument. (3) The 5-minute rest after each questionnaire is dropped; the 1-minute grey screen before each picture is kept. (4) The pictures are cut from the published figure (about 1030 × 540 px each), and the panel letters a/b/c are covered with neighbouring foliage. (5) English wording; instructions paraphrased. A question on professional training is added because the paper recruited students without it.",
